  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1029 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA pattern shift begins to develop on Sunday as an open trough\ndigging out of the PACNW begins to scoop tropical moisture into\nthe southwesterly flow over the Southwest. Temperatures will\nremain near normal Sunday afternoon, but skies will cloud over\nas upper level moisture fills in. A few isolated showers could\ndevelop on the mountains Sunday afternoon, but measurable rain\nwill remain in short supply as we wait on the deeper tropical\nmoisture to develop Sunday night. PWAT values surge in excess of\n200 percent of normal Sunday night for the Four Corners and much\nof southwest CO. With the jet overhead providing some lift, we\nshould see rain develop across much of the region by daybreak\nMonday. This will begin a wet 48 hours for our CWA as the\nremnant moisture from multiple tropical storms gets entrained\ninto the southwesterly jet streak aloft.\n\nWith this jet overhead Monday, forcing and moisture will work in\nconcert to produce training showers over much of southeast UT\nand southwest CO. The best rainfall rates appear to remain\nconfined to areas south and east of the Colorado Basin for\neastern UT and south of the Uncompahgre Plateau/San Juans. Areas\nto the north will get some wetting rain, but flash flood\nconcerns trend better to the south. The upper low begins to lift\nout of the region on Tuesday, taking much of the moisture with\nit. However, convection around the low will likely continue the\nthreat for flash flooding on rain soaked soils. \n\nHigh pressure returns to the region Wednesday and begins to dry\nthings out in the atmosphere, with temperatures returning back\ntowards normal values Thursday and Friday. Models begin to hint\nat a clipper system dragging through northern CO next weekend.\nThis could produce some cooler conditions for the northern\nmountains, depending on the evolution of this disturbance.",
